WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.333
(heartfelt piano music)

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:09.873
- My mom is the bomb.

00:00:09.999 --> 00:00:12.520
We're more like best friends.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:15.170
We're just always goofing off, we love to hang out together.

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:16.473
Like, that's my girl.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:17.900
- My mom is a person.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.129
She's vivacious, she just steps into a room,

00:00:20.129 --> 00:00:21.860
and the whole world lights up.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:24.260
She's one of the most important people in my life.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:26.144
- My mom is the strongest person I know.

00:00:26.144 --> 00:00:28.518
She's a single mom, raised my sister and I, and

00:00:28.518 --> 00:00:31.370
has been there through a lot of hard times, for

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:33.150
both my sister and I, especially recently.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.840
- I used to just be next to her when

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:37.483
she would be washing the dishes, and that would

00:00:37.483 --> 00:00:39.930
be my favorite time because I would just get

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:41.781
to talk and tell her all the gossip in school, and

00:00:41.781 --> 00:00:45.510
talk about nothing, really, but she was always there.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:46.870
- We just have a good time together.

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:47.800
We're always laughing.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:49.820
I can't even remember a time where I've

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:51.390
actually gotten into an argument

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:52.853
with her, or been mad at her.

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:55.600
We just don't have a relationship like that.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.590
- I can tell her absolutely anything.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:00.280
She has been my best friend since day one.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:01.570
- We are really close.

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:02.560
We talk everyday.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.110
If I don't hear from my mom in a day,

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.930
or in two days, then it's a weird thing.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:08.870
- She always pushed me my hardest, but she

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:12.580
also had such a soft spot for me.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:14.180
- My mom called me one day when I was

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:16.887
at work and said, "Don't freak out; I'm sure it's

00:01:16.887 --> 00:01:19.573
"no big deal, but I have a lump in my neck."

00:01:19.573 --> 00:01:21.700
- So, the first time was breast cancer.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:23.830
The second time was ovarian, the third

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:26.040
time was skin, so it's been a long road.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:27.919
I think being a single mom, it's that

00:01:27.919 --> 00:01:29.797
much harder to bring bad news to

00:01:29.797 --> 00:01:31.853
your children, and kinda scare them for the future.

00:01:31.853 --> 00:01:33.417
- There were no signs of anything when she

00:01:33.417 --> 00:01:36.996
had mammogram, something just didn't feel normal.

00:01:36.996 --> 00:01:41.092
- She flew to Taiwan and we heard from our mom and our

00:01:41.092 --> 00:01:44.393
dad over the telephone that she had cervical cancer.

00:01:44.393 --> 00:01:45.920
- She couldn't bring herself to say

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:48.064
anything, it just, "Hi, honey."

00:01:48.064 --> 00:01:50.730
and I'm like, "What?"

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:54.213
Like, no, it's like she's my mom.

00:01:54.445 --> 00:01:57.622
- You know that cancer happens to any and

00:01:57.622 --> 00:01:59.315
everybody, but when it happens

00:01:59.315 --> 00:02:01.572
to you, it's like, "Oh my god."

00:02:01.572 --> 00:02:03.828
And then to happen to my mother, my best friend,

00:02:03.828 --> 00:02:06.740
it was something I wasn't ready for and I know

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:07.830
she wasn't ready for it either.

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:10.084
- I don't really remember how I felt, I

00:02:10.084 --> 00:02:14.940
just remember being kind of like, "What do we do now?"

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:18.053
- My first reaction, though, was: "I'm going home."

00:02:18.053 --> 00:02:19.467
"I need to drop everything and"

00:02:19.467 --> 00:02:21.570
"go help her in her time of need."

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:24.290
- Just watching her go through it was very emotional

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:27.490
because I felt like I couldn't do anything for her.

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:28.890
- Everything happens for a reason, which

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:30.600
is a big mantra in my family.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.390
I don't know how much you want me to go into this.

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:33.540
I was on leave 'cause I just

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:35.190
got diagnosed with breast cancer.

00:02:35.649 --> 00:02:38.253
So, definitely learned a lot from this.

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:39.650
I don't know that I would've handled

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:41.500
all of this as well, if I hadn't had her

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:42.887
by my side for every point in my...

00:02:42.887 --> 00:02:46.943
Every surgery, every doctor's visit, and test result.

00:02:48.178 --> 00:02:50.813
I wasn't sure if I was even gonna talk about myself today.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:52.460
You roll with the punches.

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:55.629
- My sister, my dad, and I were all in

00:02:55.629 --> 00:02:58.095
the same room when it happened.

00:02:58.095 --> 00:03:01.743
It was actually more peaceful than I thought it would be.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:06.733
I had eighteen amazing years with my mom.

00:03:06.768 --> 00:03:10.670
I feel like sometimes when things go well, someone's

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:13.653
gotta be looking out for me, and it's probably her, so.

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:16.867
- I just wanted her to know that, "I'm with you."

00:03:16.867 --> 00:03:18.307
"I can't feel the pain that you're"

00:03:18.307 --> 00:03:19.860
"going through, but I'm with you."

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.553
In June, my mom will be three years breast cancer free.

00:03:22.553 --> 00:03:26.550
She's stronger mentally now because of it, and physically.

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:28.660
- My mom is doing really well.

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:32.542
She's actually healthier now than she was before cancer.

00:03:32.542 --> 00:03:35.391
- Know that you're not alone in this process.

00:03:35.391 --> 00:03:36.902
Unfortunately, there are a lot

00:03:36.902 --> 00:03:39.400
of people who are battling with cancer.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:41.800
Just know that you're stronger than what you think you are.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:43.151
- Support systems are everything.

00:03:43.151 --> 00:03:44.753
You know, people think it's cliche to say, like,

00:03:44.753 --> 00:03:46.600
"Let me know if I can do anything."

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:48.020
And there's really nothing necessarily

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:49.434
you can do, except just show your support,

00:03:49.434 --> 00:03:51.559
and show that you're there, even for something

00:03:51.559 --> 00:03:54.324
as simple as a phone call, or coffee, just to

00:03:54.324 --> 00:03:56.526
let them know that they have a

00:03:56.526 --> 00:03:59.440
shoulder to lean on is really helpful.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:02.840
- I wanna say something easy like, "Time will help."

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:07.840
But, I don't know if time has helped that much.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.300
If someone's going through this right now,

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:13.030
I hope they know that the pain that they feel,

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:15.376
it'll come in waves, you know, and you

00:04:15.376 --> 00:04:20.083
just have to ride the waves sometimes, I guess.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:22.290
- Mom, I just wanna say that

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:23.965
I love you, and I'm proud of you.

00:04:23.965 --> 00:04:26.849
The journey still continues, but you got this.

00:04:26.849 --> 00:04:31.023
- You are the strongest, most badass person, and

00:04:31.023 --> 00:04:33.096
I'm so proud to be your daughter.

00:04:33.096 --> 00:04:35.228
- Thank you for being everything.

00:04:35.228 --> 00:04:37.562
You've taught me so much about life, and

00:04:37.562 --> 00:04:39.526
persevering and especially now,

00:04:39.526 --> 00:04:41.763
I don't know where I'd be without you.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:46.443
- I really want her to know that I'm okay.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:50.163
- For anyone battling cancer, or for anyone going

00:04:50.163 --> 00:04:53.681
through this journey with someone, there are a lot

00:04:53.681 --> 00:04:57.672
of resources out there, annual walks, dancing events like

00:04:57.672 --> 00:05:00.670
dancing with survivors, and then there's also like,

00:05:00.670 --> 00:05:02.130
message boards and stuff like that.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:03.220
People you can talk to when you

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:04.830
feel like you don't have the answers.

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:07.040
You feel like you're just alone battling

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:08.553
something that you didn't ask for, so

00:05:08.553 --> 00:05:10.371
there's always people that you can talk to, there's

00:05:10.371 --> 00:05:13.485
always ways that you can help others, you just have to

00:05:13.485 --> 00:05:16.337
go to Google and search it out.

00:05:17.428 --> 00:05:20.511
^(mellow piano music)

00:05:31.498 --> 00:05:34.041
(squeaking hinges)

